      "id": "word-preparándome--getting-ready",
      "kind": "word",
      "promptEs": "preparándome",
      "answerEn": "preparing myself; getting ready",
      "noteEn": "Preparando is the ongoing-action form of preparar. The attached me identifies the speaker as the person getting ready, and the written accent preserves the word's original stress.",
      "example": { "es": "Estoy preparándome para el viaje.", "en": "I am getting ready for the trip." },
      "tags": ["word", "pronominal verb", "gerund", "attached pronoun", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-disco-duro",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "disco duro",
      "questionEn": "How do you say “hard drive” or “hard disk” in Spanish?",
      "answerEn": "Use disco duro; for more than one, use discos duros.",
      "noteEn": "This is the conventional computer-storage term. Duro describes disco, so both words take plural -s when there is more than one drive.",
      "example": { "es": "Guardé el archivo en el disco duro.", "en": "I saved the file on the hard drive." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "technical expression", "computer", "storage", "A2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "phrase-prepararse-para",
      "kind": "phrase",
      "promptEs": "prepararse para + situación o acción",
      "questionEn": "How do you say that someone gets ready for a later event, situation, or action?",
      "answerEn": "Use the matching form of prepararse, then para, then what the person is getting ready for.",
      "noteEn": "The short self-directed word changes with the person: me preparo means “I get ready,” te preparas is informal singular “you get ready,” and se prepara can mean “he, she, or formal you gets ready.” Para introduces the later event or action. With an ongoing -ando form, the short word can attach to the end, as in preparándome.",
      "example": { "es": "Nos preparamos para el examen.", "en": "We get ready for the exam." },
      "tags": ["phrase", "pronominal verb", "preparation", "A2"]
    },
